ng>Garrett Wilson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 GB hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Breece Hall</strong> is a bell-cow (66% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.50 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p>In the red zone NYJ throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to GB \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>GB \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>GB are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>36%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 6-point favorite projected to lead for 40% of snaps, <strong>GB</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>12 carries for 50 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as GB salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Christian Watson</strong> (5 targets, 52 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when GB lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 48 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYJ \u2014 a 